    "subject": "Construction Engineering",
    "chapter": "Construction Estimating",
    "topic": "Methods of Taking Out Quantities",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<ul>\r\n<li>Long wall-short wall method</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Long Walls: Long walls are those walls that run in the direction of the applied lateral load. They are typically designed to resist a significant portion of the lateral force. These are usually the primary load-resisting elements.</li>\r\n<li>Short Walls: Short walls are perpendicular to the long walls. They are designed to resist a smaller portion of the lateral force and often serve as the secondary load-resisting elements.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<li>Centre line method</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Partly centre line and partly cross wall method</li>\r\n<li>Detailed estimate</li>\r\n<li>Factors to be considered while preparing detailed estimate </li>\r\n<li>Data </li>\r\n<li>Fixing of rate per unit of an item</li>\r\n<li>Methods of preparation of approximate estimate</li>\r\n<li>Plinth area method</li>\r\n<li>Cubical contents method</li>\r\n</ul>",
