    "discipline": "Civil",
    "subject": "Construction Engineering",
    "chapter": "Project Controls",
    "topic": "Scheduling",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n    <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n<h5><span><b>PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Techniques): </b></span></h5>\r\n<p>PERT is a project management technique used in Construction Engineering to schedule, coordinate, and manage complex projects effectively. PERT focuses on analyzing the critical path and sequencing of project activities to optimize project timelines and resource allocation. It involves breaking down project tasks into smaller components, estimating the time required for each activity, and identifying dependencies between tasks. By incorporating uncertainty and variability into activity durations, PERT allows project managers to develop realistic project schedules and contingency plans. PERT diagrams visually represent project workflows, highlighting critical paths and slack time to identify areas where schedule adjustments can be made to meet project deadlines efficiently. This method is particularly useful for large-scale construction projects with numerous interdependent activities and variable task durations. Understanding PERT enables civil engineers to plan and execute construction projects systematically while effectively managing project risks and uncertainties.</p>\r\n\r\n   Three time estimates are made in PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Techniques): \r\n<ul>\r\n<li><b>Optimistic time (t$_o$)</b>. This is the minimum possible time in which an activity can be completed under the most ideal conditions. </li>\r\n<li><b>Pessimistic time (t$_p$)</b>: This is the maximum time required to complete an activity under the worst possible conditions. </li>\r\n<li><b>Most likely time (t$_m$)</b>: This required to complete an activity under normal working conditions. – Its value lies between t$_o$ and t$_p$. – It is near to the expected time</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$\\mu_{ij} = \\dfrac{a_{ij} + 4b_{ij} + c_{ij}}{6}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 428</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n<p><b>Standard deviation</b> of an activity: is the measurement of uncertainty, which is approximately one sixth of time range. Square of standard deviation is Variance of an activity.</p>\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$\\sigma_{ij} = \\dfrac{c_{ij} - a_{ij}}{6}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 428</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n<p><b>Critical Path:</b> The time wise longest path is the critical path. In this path, any type of delay in any event will cause delay in the project. These are shown by double lines or dark lines in a network.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Gantt Chart:</b></span></h5>\r\n<p>A Gantt chart is a visual project management tool used to schedule, track, and manage tasks over time. It displays a horizontal timeline divided into intervals, typically days, weeks, or months, representing the project duration. Tasks or activities are represented as bars along the timeline, with their length indicating their duration and their position showing their start and end dates. Gantt charts provide a clear overview of project timelines, dependencies between tasks, and progress tracking. They enable project managers and team members to plan resources efficiently, identify bottlenecks, adjust schedules, and communicate project status effectively.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n <center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Ecss-project-phases.png\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecss-project-phases.png\">Abrev</a>, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
