    "discipline": "Civil",
    "subject": "Engineering Economics",
    "chapter": "Time Value of Money",
    "topic": "Discounted Cash Flow Factors",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n   <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   <h5><span><b>Types of Cash Flows:</b></span></h5>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Single Cash Flow in Present (P): These are represented by a point load at the beginning of the timeline. Examples include, purchasing equipment by immediate payment of cash now.</li>\r\n<li>Single Cash Flow in Future (F): These are represented by a point load at the end of the timeline. Examples include bonds maturing in a few years, salvage value of an equipment.</li>\r\n<li>Uniform Cash Flow (A): These are represented by uniformaly distributed load. Examples include uniform depreciation, annual maintenance contracts, etc.</li>\r\n<li>Gradient Cash Flow (G): These are represented by triangular loads. These include gradually increasing (or decreasing) cashflows. Examples include increased maintenance of equipment as it gets older.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<img src=\"https://1drv.ms/i/c/9c913ae725afdeb1/IQQr6U_NS6CQQJ6Pk7y2xc2mAeNvNOWe2nmS6qjt9aaGjio?width=480\" width=\"480\" height=\"auto\" />\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Net Present Value:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<p>NPV is simply the sum of the DCF’s. Generally when choosing between two projects, the one with the higher NPV must be chosen, but it is important to compare the projects based on the same term, discount rate, and risk However, the project term, discount rate, and net cash flows can each be difficult to predict in some cases due to necessary assumptions that may prove to be inaccurate later on. The discount rate can be manipulated to reflect the relative risk of each project to help evaluate them.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Internal Rate of Return:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<p>The IRR is the discount rate for a project which results in an NPV of 0, or the case of breaking even. Generally, if the actual discount rate of a project is below the IRR then a project will be profitable. However, if the cash flows change in such a way that the NPV’s fluctuate between positive and negative values over the course of the project (non-normal cash flows), then IRR becomes ineffective. IRR can be calculated by trial and error, iterations, directly, or using a spreadsheet, but mainly depends on the method used to discount the cash flows.</p>",
    "important": "\\[FV = PV \\times (1 + i)^n\\]\r\n\r\n\\[PV = \\dfrac{FV}{(1 + i)^n}\\]",
    "resources": "<div class=\"mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bjI4NsKfEYOUtN4t6nmCT73RWwr50t3l/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-900/40 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-blue-200 transition-colors\">📄 Homework</a><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uAEUYNr8Ji9JHTaj2SDqJqowUYCrzdL4/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/40 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-green-200 transition-colors\">✅ Solution</a><!-- <div class=\"w-full mt-2\"><div style=\"padding:56.33% 0 0 0;position:relative;\"><iframe src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/1012947350?h=f495016dbb&amp;badge=0&amp;autopause=0&amp;player_id=0&amp;app_id=58479\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture; clipboard-write\" style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\" title=\"Discounted Cash Flow Factors\"></iframe></div><script src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/api/player.js\"></script></div> --></div>",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": [],
    "video": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ae0Z3pFzTNdNh6fvWQJiGPTjn9r__q-T/preview",
