    "discipline": "Civil",
    "subject": "Ethics and Professional Practice",
    "chapter": "Codes of Ethics",
    "topic": "NCEES Model Law",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<p>In our daily life, our choices and actions are a direct results of what we have been taught by parents, school, religion and society. We sometimes compare our decisions based on some loosely defined standards, which we sometime refer as values. Honesty, hardwork, integrity are examples of values. We are guided by these moral values. </p>\r\n<p>As a professional engineer, we are governed by general safety of community and we abide by our rules and regulations imposed by local and federal authorities. When these rules are insufficient or vague, we adhere the justification based on our morals and values. However, since different people are coming from different environment, their loose definition of morals can be different. Some common framework to adhere in the professional life of engineers is hence required. This is often referred as Codes of Ethics.</p>\r\n\r\nIn this section, we will address three categories:\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Obligation to Society:</li>\r\n<li>Obligation to Employers and Clients:</li>\r\n<li>Obligation to other licensees:</li>\r\n</ol>",
