    "discipline": "Civil",
    "subject": "Fluid Mechanics",
    "chapter": "Flow Measurement",
    "topic": "Weir",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>A weir is basically an obstruction in the flow path in an open channel.</li>\r\n<li>The weir will cause an increase in the water depth as the water flows over the weir.</li>\r\n<li>Greater the flow rate, greater will be increase in the depth of flow.</li>\r\n<li>The height of water above the top of weir is the measurement usually used to correlate with the flow rate.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Weir_at_Menacuddle_Well.jpg\" alt=\"Weir\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"><br/>\r\n\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weir_at_Menacuddle_Well.jpg\">Flappy Pigeon</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": []
