    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n   <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   According to NCEES Policy, mentioned in the website: NCEES.org,\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Casio: All fx-115 and fx-991 models (Any Casio calculator must have “fx-115” or “fx-991” in its model name.)</li>\r\n<li>Hewlett Packard: The HP 33s and HP 35s models, but no others</li>\r\n<li>Texas Instruments: All TI-30X and TI-36X models (Any Texas Instruments calculator must have “TI-30X” or “TI-36X” in its model name.)</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nThings you should be comfortable with your calculator:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Matrix Multiplication, Inverse and determinants</li>\r\n<li>Regression calculations: such as getting a and b coefficients for regression line equation, correlation coefficient</li>\r\n<li>Statistical Calculations: Getting Mean, Median and Mode</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>There are several Youtube videos available for doing a specific task using specific calculator. e.g. If you are using Casio 991-ES model, search for a specific task such as \"Regression Calculation using Casio 991\". If you like somone's explanation style, visit his/her channel. Most likely that person has explained the other tasks too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> If you are still unable to calculate something, let us know and we can arrange a session on calculator usage using a specific model.</p>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Scientific_calculator_2.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scientific_calculator_2.jpg\">Lhekha</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>.",
