    "discipline": "Civil",
    "subject": "Water Resources and Environmental Engineering",
    "chapter": "Water and Wastewater Treatment",
    "topic": "Softening",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n    <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   Why to soften  water and by what methods? \r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Domestic Use: Water softening is commonly used in households to improve the quality of water for bathing, cleaning, and preventing scale buildup in appliances.</li>\r\n<li>Industrial Processes: Industries often require softened water to prevent scaling and corrosion in equipment and to improve product quality.</li>\r\n<li>Boiler and Cooling Water Systems: Softening water in these systems helps maintain their efficiency and prolong their lifespan.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\nWater Softening Methods:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Ion Exchange (Water Softener):</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Principle: Ion exchange is the most common method of water softening. It involves replacing calcium and magnesium ions with sodium ions using a resin bed.</li>\r\n<li>Process: Hard water passes through a resin tank where the resin beads are charged with sodium ions. The calcium and magnesium ions are exchanged for sodium ions, effectively softening the water.</li>\r\n<li>Regeneration: The resin bed requires periodic regeneration with a brine (sodium chloride) solution to restore its sodium ions for the ion exchange process.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<li>Lime-Soda Softening:</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Principle: Lime-soda softening is a chemical precipitation process that involves adding calcium hydroxide (lime) and sodium carbonate (soda ash) to hard water to form calcium carbonate (CaCO₃) and magnesium hydroxide (Mg(OH)₂) precipitates.</li>\r\n<li>Process: The precipitates are then removed by sedimentation and filtration, resulting in softened water.</li>\r\n<li>Advantages: This method not only softens the water but also raises the pH and removes other impurities.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<li>Reverse Osmosis:</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Principle: Reverse osmosis is a filtration process that can remove hardness ions and other impurities from water.</li>\r\n<li>Process: Water is forced through a semi-permeable membrane that traps ions and impurities, producing softened water on the other side.</li>\r\n<li>Application: Reverse osmosis is effective for producing purified drinking water and can provide a degree of water softening.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</ul>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Badger_Water_Softener_Co.%2C_display_at_Nama_National_Convention%2C_Milwaukee%2C_Wisconsin_%2877704%29.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badger_Water_Softener_Co.,_display_at_Nama_National_Convention,_Milwaukee,_Wisconsin_(77704).jpg\">&quot;Tichnor Quality Views,&quot; Reg. U. S. Pat. Off. Made Only by Tichnor Bros., Inc., Boston, Mass.</a>, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
